{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Seven weeks you shall count for yourself.</b> The section mentions three festivals in their order, which are Pesah, Shavuot, and Sukkot, on which all Israel gather, and it does not mention Rosh HaShanah or Yom HaKippurim, where they do not gather at the Holy Temple (bet hamikdash). From this it is that we do not say in the Musaf prayer of Rosh HaShanah and Yom HaKippurim 'and may we ascend and appear,' as we say in the prayers of the three festivals, but rather 'and we are unable to perform our obligations.' And the fact that it says תספר לך — 'you shall count' — in the singular, while in another place (Leviticus 23:16) it says תספרו — 'you shall count' — fifty days in the plural: it is the way of Scripture to mention the commandments sometimes in the singular and sometimes in the plural, and after mentioning the commandments in the plural it returns and mentions them in the singular, so that no individual will think to exempt himself from it by means of the collective and escape its punishment; therefore it returns and addresses each and every individual. As in the matter of the commandment of tzitzit, which is stated in the plural (Numbers 15:38): ״ועשו להם ציצית על כנפי בגדיהם לדורותם ונתנו על ציצית הכנף פתיל תכלת״, and it returned and mentioned it in the singular (Deuteronomy 22:12): ״גדילים תעשה לך על ארבע כנפות כסותך״. And it goes without saying regarding a single commandment in many verses, that even in a single verse we find plural and singular, as it is written (Leviticus 19:9): ״ובקצרכם את קציר ארצכם לא תכלה פאת שדך״ — and so forth. And there is another reason: had the commandment been written in the plural and not in the singular, I would say that the commandment is said only to the court (bet din) and not every individual among Israel is obligated in it; therefore Scripture needed to return and mention it in the singular. And likewise with the commandment of lulav it mentioned it in the plural (Leviticus 23:40): ״ולקחתם לכם ביום הראשון״, and the singular is not mentioned anywhere — yet it says there ״ושמחתם לפני ה' אלהיכם״, and it is impossible to understand that the commandment should be addressed to the court and not to all Israel, for all Israel are obligated in the rejoicing of the festivals; and we have already found in the singular: ״ושמחת בחגך״. And regarding the matter of counting, the Sages, blessed be their memory, said: שבעה שבועות תספר לך — there is a commandment to count the days and a commandment to count the weeks. There are those who hold thus: a commandment to count the days each and every day, and a commandment to count the weeks upon the completion of the weeks, but not every day, for there is no need to mention the weeks at every particular of the days — and this is the opinion of the Ma'or, of blessed memory. And there are those who hold: a commandment to count the days and a commandment to count the weeks each and every day; once the first week has passed, there is a commandment to count the weeks that follow it on each and every day of the days that he counts, because the weeks allude to the seventh millennium, and therefore one must mention them every day — and this is the opinion of the sage R. E. ben Ezra." "<b>From the time the sickle is first put to the standing grain you shall begin to count.</b> It teaches you that the reaping and the counting come as one. And the Sages, blessed be their memory, interpreted: one might think he may reap and count and bring whenever he wishes — Scripture says (Leviticus 23:15): ״מיום הביאכם״; if from ״the day you bring,״ one might think he may reap and count and bring by day — Scripture says (ibid.): ״שבע שבתות תמימות תהיינה״ — when do you find them תמימות (whole/complete)? When you count from evening. How so? The reaping and the counting at night and the bringing by day. And furthermore the Sages, blessed be their memory, interpreted: do not read בקמה (standing grain) but rather בקומה (standing) — from here, that the counting of the Omer is performed standing; and just as the reaping is standing, so too the counting is standing. And there are four things whose commandment is performed standing, and their mnemonic is עצמ״ל, and they are: Omer, Tzitzit, Milah, Lulav."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:56.507442530Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}